    "problem_statement": "Dict.copy() behavior changed in latest version, now performs a deep copy instead of a shallow copy\n\nWhen using the `copy()` method on a Dict object, it now performs a deep copy instead of a shallow copy, which breaks expected behavior.\n\nMinimal example to reproduce:\n\n```python\nfrom addict import Dict\n\n# Create a nested dictionary\nd = Dict()\nd.a = {'value': 21}\n\n# Create a copy\nd_copy = d.copy()\n\n# Modify the original\nd.a['value'] = 42\n\n# Check values\nprint(d.a['value'])  # 42\nprint(d_copy.a['value'])  # Expected: 42 (for shallow copy), but now returns 21\n```\n\nThe expected behavior (and previously implemented behavior) is that `copy()` would perform a shallow copy, meaning changes to nested objects in the original would be reflected in the copy. This is consistent with Python's built-in `dict.copy()` method.\n\nNow `copy()` behaves exactly like `deepcopy()`, which is redundant and breaks backward compatibility with code that expects the standard shallow copy behavior."
  },
